## Your rights at work

Type 1 diabetes usually counts as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010**, because the law looks at how you would be **without your insulin**. Your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- **breaks** to check your glucose, eat or treat a hypo
- a **private place** to inject or change a pump site
- somewhere to **store insulin**
- time off for **appointments** and education courses
- flexibility around **shift patterns**

Most jobs are open to people with type 1 diabetes. Some roles have extra rules, for example driving buses or lorries, or flying.

## Free prescriptions

In **England**, people with diabetes who take medicine, including insulin, can get a **medical exemption certificate** for free prescriptions. Ask your GP surgery for the form. Prescriptions are **free for everyone in Wales**.

## Driving

If you take insulin, you **must tell the DVLA**. See [living well with type 1 diabetes](/conditions/physical/type-1-diabetes/living-well/) for the key rules.

## Benefits for children

Children with type 1 diabetes often need a lot of extra care, including checks and insulin during the day and at night. Because of this, many families claim **Disability Living Allowance (DLA)** for their child. It is based on how much **extra care** your child needs compared with other children the same age.

{% checker tool="dla" /%}

## Benefits for adults

**PIP** is based on how your condition affects your daily life. Many adults manage type 1 diabetes independently, and they usually do not qualify on diabetes alone. You may qualify if you:

- need **prompting or supervision** to manage insulin or check your glucose, for example because of frequent hypos or hypo unawareness
- have **complications**, such as sight loss, nerve damage in your feet or kidney disease, that affect daily tasks or walking

{% checker tool="pip" /%}
